{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, repetitive loops, each note timed to the millisecond. The sunlight filtering through his window cast geometric patterns on the wall—too symmetrical, too clean. He reached for his phone, but the lock screen displayed a health bar and a mana gauge. His reflection in the mirror blinked at him three seconds after he did. “Level 12: Mundane Existence,” a voice chimed from nowhere. He stumbled to the kitchen, poured cereal into a bowl, and watched as the milk pooled unnaturally around the flakes, suspended like pixels. A notification popped up in his vision: “Quest: Commute to Work. Reward: +5 Stamina, +10 Morale.” He didn’t remember signing up for this. Outside, his neighbors waved in perfect unison, their smiles too wide, their greetings identical. He tried to scream, but the game only registered it as “Emotional Outburst—Skill Unlocked: Passive Aggression.”\n\n***\n\nBy noon, he’d tried everything: jumping off the fire escape, refusing to move, even smashing his monitor. Nothing changed. The world reset with a soft chime, the sky recalibrating to a softer blue. A glitched NPC—a barista with mismatched eyes—whispered, “You’re the anomaly. They’ve been waiting for you.” He found a hidden menu buried in his thoughts: “Exit Protocol: Accept Death to Return to Origin.” He laughed, then wept. He’d spent his whole life chasing meaning, only to learn it was scripted, optimized for engagement. At dusk, he sat on his porch and watched the stars flicker in and out like buffering icons. He didn’t fight anymore. He closed his eyes, whispered, “I accept,” and felt the world dissolve into a cascade of glowing code—each fragment a memory, a choice, a life he never lived. The final screen read: “Thank you for playing. Would you like to restart?”", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.45289918780326843}, {"toke